ar less in common with them than I  do with people in Montreal or people in  Madrid and and that is part of the  United States really having very  disparate cultures within it I feel like  the language piece is a big aspect so if  you grow up in the United States are  used to only speaking English yeah and  you know you say say you go to  for a month and you have a very tough  time communicating with people you're  gonna get some culture shock from that  yeah but I feel like you get used to  that yeah after a while you get used to  the fact that people speak a different  language doesn't mean you understand it  you might start picking up a few words  but I do think that as far as the shock  element you were just - that I after  spending several days in in northern  Humboldt County or after spending time  in rural northern Indiana as I got to do  about six or eight months ago  the culture differences don't evaporate  after a week whereas I get used to  hearing Dutch or French or whatever  after a few days in a European city I  wouldn't say that I've experienced  culture shock myself but in talking to  people it sounds like people tend to get  it coming back to their home country oh  that's rather than visiting and I think  that's what you expressed that's that's  pretty interesting  yeah yeah I'm open to hearing from  people and I've never traveled to Asia  and I know that there are folks who have  traveled to Asia who described culture  shock is much more significant and I'm  hoping to visit Asia in the next few  years but I'd be sort of open to that  being a different experience send me  your thoughts you've got a great bonus  show for you today  two ways to access it WWN pakman.com or  patreon.com slash david pakman show both  give you access to the supporter only  world-famous bonus show
 

